  "sourceJudgments": [
    {
      "page": 24,
      "finding": "Viola and Cello have separate musical phrases; surviving right-boundary connectivity is structural. Viola low cresc./forte/hairpins and Cello high closing half notes/sharp/slur occupy overlapping vertical bands. Both full neighboring cores can be excluded, but a single full-width rectangle cannot remove every foreign mark."
    },
    {
      "page": 28,
      "finding": "The first internal barline connects the two violin rows. Adjacent violin noteheads, their own stems/flag and upper/lower slurs are distinct from that physical boundary. Full first and second violin cores can be separated; preserve ViolinII low flagged note/hairpin/forte and Viola high-note/slur remnants where ranges overlap."
    },
    {
      "page": 31,
      "finding": "The right system boundary is visibly separate from the last ViolinI beam and ViolinII flagged notes, lower slur and crescendo. Local staff lines are damaged and curved; their missing fragments cannot be fabricated as full lines. Full ViolinI core can be excluded while retaining ViolinII source envelope."
    },
    {
      "page": 38,
      "finding": "The accepted body location is on a Cello tie at a continuing measureline, not a separate notehead. All Cello noteheads/ties belong to Cello; the vertical measureline does not couple them to Viola. Viola final high note/double arc overlaps the vertical range of ViolinII lower markings; perfect neighbor-fragment removal is impossible with one horizontal rectangle."
    }
  ]
